WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:02.489
Film Courage: What keeps you here now?

00:00:02.489 --> 00:00:07.789
Adam William Ward, Actor/Director:&nbsp;In Los
Angeles? I love the city and have been luck

00:00:07.789 --> 00:00:15.100
enough when I came here my brother also lived
here. Now my whole family lives here. My sister

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.980
is here (my younger sister) and my parents
are in Palm Desert, it’s not far. So family

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.330
in a way will keep me here no matter what.
But I love Los Angeles, it’s such a sprawled

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:32.070
out city. There are all of these different
pockets of cool things to do or hikes or whatever

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.720
and the weather is beautiful. I don’t try
to promote it. I never try to get more people

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.500
to move here because so many people move here
already.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.170
Film Courage: But a lot of people move back.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:47.040
Adam:&nbsp;Back home, yes…a lot of people give
up and move away. Because of the weather I

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.790
feel like a lot of people move to Los Angeles.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:57.700
But I’m in the mecca of moviemaking in the
United States. That’s where Los Angeles

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.360
is so I don’t know what kinds of trials
and tribulations you’d have if you were

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.780
in Atlanta or somewhere else. Maybe there’s
enough where you would have enough film people.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:15.380
But here in my phone I have 10 DPs I can call
(Directors of Photography) and they own a

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.530
camera. Here there are so many people that
are making films, there are so many people

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:26.990
that own cameras, it’s really saturated.
To get a project off of the ground, it’s

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.670
a lot easier.
As to where in Middle America, do you know

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.310
anyone who owns a Red camera or Arri Alexa
or any of these kinds of cameras. It’s probably

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.840
really hard to find someone who owns one in
Nebraska or some place.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:49.090
Here you’re in a bar, somebody in the bar
probably owns a camera. It’s so saturated

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.150
in that way.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.810
Film Courage: Hopefully they are sober enough
to operate it?

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.680
Adam:&nbsp;Well…they’re not going on set like
that. But when you meet them, it might help.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.780
Film Courage: Right. But do you have a goal
list? Do you have a certain amount of things

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.060
where by this date I want to have these many
things completed?

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:10.439
Adam: I mean I want to make 30 movies in my
life probably. It’s whatever inspires me

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:17.099
really like in my phone I have ideas for movies
that go on and on. I must have 20 or 30 ideas

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:28.549
for features. Some are fleshed out and some
are really raw (just one or two sentences).

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:34.069
So I came here and I worked for DreamWorks
and I worked for for Todd Phillips and I realized

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:40.560
(it’s funny) the people around me want to
be directors, they want to be some position

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.312
in film or they want to be some position in
film and they weren’t doing it yet. And

00:02:42.312 --> 00:02:46.049
they weren’t doing in yet and I was like
“How long have you worked here?” “Oh,

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:51.969
I’ve been doing this 11 years and I want
to be a film director.” And I was like “So

00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:56.669
I could be you in 11 years? Yeah…I’ve
got to stop.” So I left Todd Phillips’

00:02:56.669 --> 00:03:01.749
office. I was like I want to act and direct.
So I went to acting school and I started acting

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:09.629
in every possible thing I could. I did 11
short films in 2009 and then I was the lead

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:16.340
in 3 independent feature films in 2010 and
when I saw the independent feature films,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.400
I realized they weren’t good enough and
I said to myself “I have to get behind the

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.859
camera if I want to make my dreams come true.”

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:26.079
And I always planned on getting behind the
camera eventually but it just kind of pushed

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:32.269
me to do it sooner so I thought I was just
going to produce and act and I manage to raise

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:38.559
the money together to film a TV pilot and
we were going to produce. We had a director

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:43.739
and everybody. The director pulled out and
it was his script. So I told the money people

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:48.140
“Hold on! I’ll write something else and
we can shoot that.” So I ended up writing

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:54.119
THREE GUYS AND A COUCH which is a TV pilot
that I wrote and ended up directing because

00:03:54.119 --> 00:03:58.249
I couldn’t find a director. Everybody couldn’t
see the vision that I had so I ended up directing

00:03:58.249 --> 00:04:04.540
it to. So I acted, directed and wrote it.
We filmed it in 2011. It’s actually on Amazon

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:12.359
Prime (you can go watch it on Amazon Prime)
and it’s a comedy pilot (it’s a 20-minute

00:04:12.359 --> 00:04:13.359
pilot).

00:04:13.359 --> 00:04:19.889
I loved Seinfeld growing up and so it was
kind of my newer version of Seinfeld. My best

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:24.949
version of it I guess that I could do. And
it’s just about three guys trying to rent

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:30.370
out their couch to make rent. So the whole
episode is couch interviews, they are interviewing

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.689
people to rent the couch.

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:38.190
That was the pilot episode and I have in my
phone like 50 episodes of that show. I just

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:42.620
kept writing ideas about that show and that
show. So hopefully I do another show and use

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.830
all of those ideas eventually.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:53.759
But I did that TV pilot. It was the first
thing I ever directed from that pilot someone

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:58.680
at Fox saw it and Fox called me in and was
like “Hey…” (It was a subsidiary of

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.379
Fox but was on the Fox lot) called me in and
was like “Hey, we’d really like to develop

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:07.071
a new show with you because it was really
good for your first thing so let’s develop

00:05:07.071 --> 00:05:08.250
something else.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:13.491
So I came in every week for like 8 or 9 months
developing new ideas with them for new ideas

00:05:13.491 --> 00:05:18.470
for new shows eventually settling on an idea
for a show called PAROLE OFFICERS which is

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:23.860
about a guy who gets arrested and he gets
two parole officers that are going to help

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:28.680
him change his life back around but he got
falsely arrested for bad things so he really

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.430
has no problems like that and the two parole
officers are completely crazy who take over

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:37.210
his life and destroy it even more.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:44.611
So we shot that in 2009…whoah…hold up…rewind…Fox
ended up scheduling for me to do a table read.

00:05:44.611 --> 00:05:47.280
They were going like we’re going to give
you a million dollar budget to do the pilot

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.319
(all that kind of stuff) and then they ended
up pulling the plug. And so the day before

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:57.030
the table read they were like “We have something
similar, we’re not going to do the table

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.030
read.”

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:04.199
What they did not know is that every time
I would come in with the script I was copyrighting

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:09.110
it so I knew that I owned the copyright on
the show. So okay you have something similar

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.370
but you’re not going to steal my idea.”

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:16.259
I was like heartbroken for awhile. It was
hard to get over. It was 8 or 9 months of

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:21.439
me telling my friends or whatever that I’m
doing a show with Fox. So for me I was very

00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:24.470
heartbroken when it didn’t happen but then
I was like “You know what, I’m going to

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:25.710
film it myself.”

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:31.499
So THREE GUYS AND THE COUCH we filmed for
$5,000 at the time on the Red Camera. So this

00:06:31.499 --> 00:06:36.129
I also ended up filming for $5,000 myself.
It’s a 27-minute pilot (it’s also on Amazon

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:41.699
Prime - PAROLE OFFICERS) and so I ended up
raising the money independently and we filmed

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:45.659
the pilot as well in 2013.

00:06:45.659 --> 00:06:52.879
From there I ended up directing two short
films for friends and then it was like it’s

00:06:52.879 --> 00:06:56.620
time to do a feature so that’s when WALLY
GOT WASTED GOT MADE.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:02.819
Film Courage: I’m hoping we can go back
to when you worked at Todd Phillips’ office

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:08.630
and at what point did you have that “Wow?
I could be this person here or this person

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:13.380
here. And they seem happy with their job…or
maybe they’re not…maybe they are? But

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:19.499
I don’t want this to be me.” Can you take
us throw how long and when you left?

00:07:19.499 --> 00:07:26.349
Adam: I think in the beginning it was just
so exciting to be there. I mean Angelina Jolie

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:30.311
would come into the office. Like Angelina
Jolie was in the hallway and I was like “Oh,

00:07:30.311 --> 00:07:32.750
there’s a pretty girl in the hallway.”
Then she’s standing out there for awhile

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:36.870
and I’m like “Maybe this woman is lost?”
She was pregnant at the time. I’m like “Maybe

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:40.490
this pregnant woman is lost?” So I go out
to the hallway and I’m like “Hey, are

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.590
you okay? You look lost.” And then of course
once I get in the hallway I’m like “Oh!

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.150
I’m talking to Angelina Jolie.” And she’s
like “No, I’m just waiting and I’m going

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.750
to go up to a meeting upstairs and I just
wanted a moment by myself. And I’m like

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.830
“Oh, okay.” And then I walk back in the
office. She’s right on the other side of

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:59.170
the glass and there is no one else around
so of course I’m like “Angelina Jolie

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:02.219
is here” to the office.

00:08:02.219 --> 00:08:06.529
And then the guy who played HELLBOY at the
time, he was coming into the office and I

00:08:06.529 --> 00:08:11.930
met so many people coming into the office
that were making movies and actors. So in

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:16.389
the beginning it was just so exciting to be
there. On the post side of things I never

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:22.370
would meet movie stars…I mean I met Stephen
Spielberg and that but I wouldn’t meet Angelina

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.710
Jolie at Dreamworks at post production because
they just don’t go there. But there it was

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:30.379
just so exciting. I think it took awhile for
the excitement to wear off because I was doing

00:08:30.379 --> 00:08:35.260
script coverage on like three scripts a day.
They would expect three scripts a day for

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:41.550
me to read and do coverage on them and I’m
a slow reader too. But constantly trying to

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:46.740
break it down and write a one-page synopsis
of the whole movie and green light it or not

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:51.190
green light it. If you didn’t get through
me, the people above me never read it. So

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:54.980
that’s how it would work. So if I don’t
like the script, the person above me doesn’t

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:01.880
like the script…they are never going to
read the script. Which was kind of crazy to

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.350
me in a way because these people have agents
or managers but if you don’t get it through

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:12.800
the guy at the front desk, you don’t to
Scott or Tod or anyone else. So I am literally

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.019
like green lighting someone’s life or not.
But if I send it up the ladder and it’s

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:22.850
not good, now I’m not considered a very
good script coverage guy. So my own behind

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.880
is on the line. It was very interesting at
the time.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:34.350
I think it took three months before the excitement
of it all wore off and I started asking more

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.029
questions.

00:09:36.029 --> 00:09:42.380
Tod’s assistant at the time was his assistant
for 9 years and I found out he was a film

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:46.230
director and I saw some commercials he shot
and some other stuff. He was like “Yeah,

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.800
I want to be a film director.” And I’m
like “But you’ve been his assistant for

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:58.060
9 years? Clearly he’s not going to help
you make a film. He’s not going to help

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:01.029
me make a film. I’ve got to go make a film
on my own.”

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:07.740
So that’s the epiphany that I definitely
had. If you’re not doing the job that you’re

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.300
supposed to be doing, they can’t see. Like
something that I’ve learned in Hollywood

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.670
since I’ve been here is that people don’t
see potential. It’s not something that they

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:18.589
see. It’s either you’ve done it or you
haven’t done it. It’s very rare that someone

00:10:18.589 --> 00:10:25.190
can see potential of what you’ve done. There’s
no excuses. You’ve either made a phenomenal

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:29.240
movie or you’ve made a mediocre movie and
they think you’re always going to make a

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.459
mediocre movie.

00:10:32.459 --> 00:10:37.300
Or I guess in any position that you’ve done.
They don’t see potential like “Oh, you

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.310
had a really good moment here or there.”
Your budget…you didn’t have enough money.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.649
They don’t see the excuses. Either it’s
good enough or it’s not.

00:10:44.649 --> 00:10:49.070
Film Courage: Why do you think that is? Do
you think because everything goes so fast

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:52.860
and there is too much money on the line or
they don’t have time to nurture something?

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:56.090
Adam: I think it might have been different
back in the day. I think they might have nurtured

00:10:56.090 --> 00:11:02.151
people back in the day. Like all the studios
now are corporate like Viacom, like all of

00:11:02.151 --> 00:11:07.170
these big companies own the studios. It’s
not really owned by an individual now. I think

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:10.399
back in the day Paramount (was it Paramount
I think?) was owned by a guy with a lion.

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:14.240
He would go to the premieres with a lion.
I mean it was one person’s decision and

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.700
a few people below him.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:22.269
Back in the 90’s all of the studios got
bought out by big companies. Everything is

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:26.130
powered by money and so if everything is powered
by money are you going to take a chance on

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:30.981
someone who has already delivered or someone
who has potential. If your behind is on the

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:36.019
line you are going to go with the safe bet.
So part of me understands that but I think

00:11:36.019 --> 00:11:41.430
also it takes a very special person to see
potential. I don’t think everybody can see

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:47.860
potential. It’s almost a rare skill compared
to the talent. Some people are very, very

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:54.839
talented. Some people only have that rare
gift of seeing talent too. And half the people

00:11:54.839 --> 00:12:01.459
that I meet that are at the top still they
don’t have that gift. They shove 20-40 million

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:05.620
dollars in advertising on a movie and still
that movie might fail. Well someone else might

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.190
have been able to see that it was going to
fail but they couldn’t see it but they have

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.800
the title of head of the studio or whatever.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:16.069
So it’s just interesting to see how certain
things happen or whatever. I know people in

00:12:16.069 --> 00:12:20.640
positions who tell me all kinds of stories
where I’m like “Really, I’ve saw the

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.779
trailer to this movie. I have no idea what
that movie is about.” And it’s a two minute

00:12:23.779 --> 00:12:27.430
trailer. Like if I don’t know what your
movie is about and it’s a two-minute trailer,

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:32.959
it’s a really bad sign. And you’re putting
40 million dollars into advertising. That

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:35.279
wows my mind.

